<header><p class="st-kicker">electorate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Electorate of Hesse</h1><p class="st-meta">1803 CE – 1866 CE · capital Kassel</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hesse-Kassel as an electorate whose rulers kept the electoral title for sixty years after the Empire ended.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Landgrave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-elector-of-hesse">William IX of Hesse-Kassel</a>, enriched by hiring out soldiers to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, was made an elector in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss">Imperial Recess of 1803</a> and took the title Elector William I. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> ended in 1806 before he could take part in an imperial election.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> dispossessed William in 1806 and his lands became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-westphalia">Kingdom of Westphalia</a>. Restored in 1813, he cancelled the French reforms and kept the title of elector. A constitution was granted in 1831, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-hesse">Frederick William I</a> quarrelled with his parliament, leading to a crisis in 1850 that nearly brought war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, the capital, retained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergpark-wilhelmshohe">Wilhelmshöhe</a> palace park.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> of 1866. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> occupied it and annexed it, forming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-hesse-nassau">Province of Hesse-Nassau</a> with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-nassau">Duchy of Nassau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/electorate-of-hesse.jpg" alt="Electorate of Hesse" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hesse.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1803 CE – 1866 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>electorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kassel</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William I, William II, Frederick William I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hesse-Kassel, Kurhessen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1834&amp;focus=electorate-of-hesse" aria-label="Electorate of Hesse on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4895 400 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Electorate of Hesse lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5215 656L5215 656M5215 656L5215 656M5215 656L5199 691M5215 656L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5215 656h0M5215 656h0M5199 691h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5215 656h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5215 656h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kassel. <a href="/atlas?year=1834&amp;focus=electorate-of-hesse">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
